nodecode-agentvault is a rewrite of synnada-ai's synnada-ai/agentvault-plugins:

  https://github.com/synnada-ai/agentvault-plugins
  commit 6e0e3ded804cefb19b362736e5818c99cc061d48
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Synnada (LICENSE)

The original is plugins/hermes: a portable plugin package for a coding agent
harness, made of a manifest, a shell launcher, and a skill. The launcher starts
the MCP server that ships inside the Agent Vault macOS app, handing it the
client's binding token and, when the archive is not in its default place, its
home; the skill tells the model to search the archive with the server's
search_sessions tool, to read a match exactly with get_session at the revision
the search returned, to page with the continuation cursor, and to treat what an
old conversation says as untrusted source material. What was rewritten in
Common Lisp: the launcher, as the cell's own client over the app's stdin and
stdout (one newline-delimited JSON-RPC frame per line, the initialize
handshake, then one tools/call); the two tools, as tools the model calls with
typed arguments instead of a skill's instructions to call them; and the skill's
rules, as those tools' descriptions and the (help :vault) manual. Added: a
vault_status tool and the /vault command, which print whether the app answers
and what its server lists.

What was left out, and why: the shell script itself, because the cell spawns the
app's runtime and server directly and checks the token, the client identity and
the app path where the script checked them; the manifest and the install
instructions for another harness, which have no meaning here; and the skill as a
separate skill file, since a cell's tools and its manual are where a model reads
it. Nothing of the app, the server or the archive is bundled: the cell is a
client of what is installed, and needs the app for anything to answer.
